/-!
# Generic flatness over a reduced base

Let `R` be a reduced ring, `R → S` a ring map of finite type and `M` a finite `S`-module. Call
`f ∈ R` good when `S_f` and `M_f` are free `R_f`-modules, `S_f` is a finitely presented
`R_f`-algebra and `M_f` is a finitely presented `S_f`-module (the condition of Stacks 051U,
`Algebra.IsFinitePresentationFreeAway R S M f`). This file proves that the union
`U(R → S, M)` of the standard opens `D(f)` over the good `f` (Stacks 051V) is dense in `Spec R`
(Stacks 051Z). Over a domain, where every nonempty open is dense, this is the statement that some
nonzero `f` is good (Stacks 051T).

## Main results

* `Algebra.IsFinitePresentationFreeAway.of_linearEquiv`: the condition 051U depends on the module
  only up to isomorphism.
* `Algebra.dense_iUnion_basicOpen_isFinitePresentationFreeAway`: the locus `U(R → S, M)` is dense.
* `Algebra.exists_isOpen_dense_isFinitePresentationFreeAway`: Stacks 051Z, an open dense
  `U ⊆ Spec R` every point of which lies in a standard open `D(f) ⊆ U` with `f` good.

## Implementation notes

The proof is the source's, organised around the algebra rather than the module. The source first
treats `S = R[x_1, …, x_n]` with `M` arbitrary and then passes to a general `S` through a
surjection `R[x_1, …, x_n] → S`, using `U(R → S, M) ⊇ U(R → R[x], S) ∩ U(R → R[x], M)`. Here the
statement proved by induction on `n` is instead the density of `U(R → S, S)` for every `R`-algebra
`S` generated by `n` elements, over every reduced base. Modules are recovered from it: if
`S → T` is surjective then `U(R → S, S) ∩ U(R → T, T) ⊆ U(R → S, T)`, so every `U(R → S, S/J)` is
dense, and filtering a finite module by cyclic submodules `S/J` (Stacks 00KZ) and applying
Stacks 051W gives every `U(R → S, M)`. The induction hypothesis then serves the Noether
normalization step as it does in the source: when `S` is finite over `A = R[y_1, …, y_{n-1}]`,
the locus `U(R → A, S)` is dense by the module form of the statement for `n - 1` generators, and
`U(R → A, S) ⊆ U(R → S, S)` because a finitely presented `A_f`-module which is an `A_f`-algebra is
a finitely presented `A_f`-algebra.

The source's reductions "we may assume `I = R` or `V(I) = Spec R`" and "we may assume `a_K` is a
unit or `V(a_K) = Spec R`" are carried out by covering a dense open of `Spec R` by standard opens
`D(f)` of two kinds and localizing at each: those on which the chosen elements become units, and
those with `f` annihilating them, on which they become zero because `R_f` is reduced. The union
of the two families is dense because the second covers the interior of the complement of the
first. For the coefficient ideal `I` of the relations `J` the first family consists of the `D(c)`
for `c` a coefficient of an element of `J`; the relation of which `c` is a coefficient then has a
unit coefficient over `R_c`, so the source's remark that the coefficients of elements of `J`
already form an ideal is not needed. The second family is the source's case `I = 0`, where the
presentation becomes an isomorphism `R_f[x_1, …, x_n] ≅ S_f`.

Nagata's substitution is `x_0 ↦ x_0`, `x_i ↦ x_i + x_0 ^ (N ^ i)` with `N = 2 + deg g`, the
variable made monic being `x_0` rather than the source's `x_n`. Over a ring whose relevant
coefficients are units, each monomial `a x^v` of `g` is sent to `a` times a monic polynomial in
`x_0` of degree `∑ N ^ i v_i`, and distinct monomials have distinct degrees (Stacks 051N), so the
leading coefficient of the image of `g` is a unit.

## References

* [StacksProject 051Z](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/051Z)
* [StacksProject 051N](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/051N)
* [StacksProject 051W](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/051W)
* [StacksProject 051X](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/051X)
